          "claim": "Let $e=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\hat e_ie_i\\in S$ with $\\delta(e,e)=1$. Since each $M_{0i}$ lies in the linear subspace $\\mathfrak g$ by [L1], $N_e=-\\sum_{i=1}^3\\hat e_iM_{0i}$ lies in $\\mathfrak g$. From $(M_{0i})^{\\mu}{}_{\\nu}=\\delta^{\\mu}_0\\eta_{i\\nu}-\\delta^{\\mu}_i\\eta_{0\\nu}=\\delta^{\\mu}_0\\delta_{i\\nu}+\\delta^{\\mu}_i\\delta_{0\\nu}$ (using $\\eta_{i\\nu}=\\delta_{i\\nu}$ for spatial indices and $\\eta_{0\\nu}=-\\delta_{0\\nu}$) one reads $M_{0i}e_0=e_i$ and $M_{0i}e_j=\\delta_{ij}e_0$ for $j=1,2,3$, since the components vanish unless $\\mu\\in\\{0,i\\}$ and $\\nu\\in\\{0,j\\}$. Hence $N_ee_0=-\\sum_i\\hat e_ie_i=-e$ and $N_ee_j=-\\sum_i\\hat e_i\\delta_{ij}e_0=-\\hat e_je_0$, so $N_ee=-\\sum_j\\hat e_j^2e_0=-e_0$ because $\\delta(e,e)=\\sum_j\\hat e_j^2=1$. Every $v\\in V$ decomposes as $v=v^0e_0+se+y$ with $v^0,s\\in\\mathbb R$ and $y\\in S$, $\\delta(e,y)=0$: indeed [L8] gives $v=v^0e_0+w$ with $w\\in S$, and $w=\\delta(e,w)e+y$ with $y:=w-\\delta(e,w)e$ satisfies $\\delta(e,y)=\\delta(e,w)-\\delta(e,w)\\delta(e,e)=0$. For such $v$, linearity gives $N_ev=v^0(-e)+s(-e_0)+0=-v^0e-se_0$ and $N_e^2v=N_e(-v^0e-se_0)=v^0e_0+se$; in particular $N_ey=0$ and $N_e^2=P$, where $P$ is the projection onto $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,e)$ along $\\{y\\in S:\\delta(e,y)=0\\}$. This last set is the $\\eta$-orthogonal complement of $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,e)$, because $\\eta(v,v')=0$ for all $v'\\in\\operatorname{span}(e_0,e)$ holds exactly for $v\\in e_0^{\\perp}=S$ with $\\delta(e,v)=0$; and in the ordered basis $(e_0,e)$ the map $N_e$ has the matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\ -1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.",
