          "claim": "Surface form: let $G(t):=\\int_Sf(\\sigma,t)\\,dS(\\sigma)$, a finite real number for each $t$ by [F5]. Fix $s,t\\in[a,b]$ with $s\\ne t$ and put $g(\\sigma):=\\frac{f(\\sigma,s)-f(\\sigma,t)}{s-t}$; $g$ is continuous on the compact $S$, hence $\\sigma\\mapsto g(\\sigma)-\\partial_tf(\\sigma,t)$ is continuous, bounded and integrable against $dS$, and by linearity of the surface integral [F5] on the integrable functions $G(s)-G(t)=\\int_S\\bigl(f(\\sigma,s)-f(\\sigma,t)\\bigr)dS(\\sigma)=(s-t)\\int_Sg\\,dS(\\sigma)$. For every $\\sigma\\in S$ the mean value theorem [F1] supplies $\\tau$ strictly between $t$ and $s$ with $g(\\sigma)=\\partial_tf(\\sigma,\\tau)$, and again no $\\tau$ is selected as a function; uniform continuity of $\\partial_tf$ on the compact set $S\\times[a,b]$ by [F4], the finiteness of the surface measure $S(S)$ and the monotonicity and triangle inequality for the surface integral [F5] then give, for $0<\\lvert s-t\\rvert<\\delta$ with the $\\delta$ of step 3.1 applied to $\\eta:=\\varepsilon/(1+S(S))$, the pointwise bound $\\lvert g(\\sigma)-\\partial_tf(\\sigma,t)\\rvert<\\eta$ and hence $\\bigl\\lvert[G(s)-G(t)]/(s-t)-\\int_S\\partial_tf(\\sigma,t)\\,dS(\\sigma)\\bigr\\rvert=\\bigl\\lvert\\int_S\\bigl(g-\\partial_tf(\\cdot,t)\\bigr)dS\\bigr\\rvert\\le\\eta\\,S(S)<\\varepsilon$, exactly as in steps 2.1 and 3.1 with $S$ in place of $K$ and $dS$ in place of $d\\lambda_n$. Passing to the limit as in step 4.1 gives the surface form, including the relative endpoint derivatives.",
