0|x|)$ ([[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]], [[thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data]]). The pair $(E,0,\\rho,0)$ therefore realises the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with source $\\rho$, and $E$ is the field of the localised Newtonian potential. (b) [Uniqueness with vanishing at infinity] If $\\psi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfies $-\\Delta\\psi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}\\psi(x)=0$, then $\\psi=\\varphi$ ([[thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-the-laplacian]]). In particular, among $C^2$ solutions of the Poisson equation of a compactly supported $C^1$ source decaying at infinity, the localised Newtonian potential is the unique one. (c) [Compactly supported $L^1$ sources] If instead $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ is real, measurable and compactly supported, then $N\\rho$ is finite $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere, belongs to $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$, is $C^\\infty$ and harmonic off the support of $\\rho$, and its regular distribution satisfies $$-\\Delta T_{\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho}=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$ ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]], scaled by $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$; [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-distributional-derivative]])",
