     "source_section": "Statement",
     "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, polar, Newtonian and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Work in $\\mathbb R^3$ with its Euclidean structure ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-metric-ball]]) and the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}>0$. Let $\\Phi$ be the $n=3$ fundamental solution of $-\\Delta$ of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]], normalised by $-\\Delta T_\\Phi=\\delta_0$; then $\\omega_2=4\\pi$ and $$\\Phi(z)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi|z|}\\qquad(z\\ne0)$$ ([[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]). Let $Nf$ be the Newtonian potential of [[def-newtonian-potential]]. (a) [Smooth compact sources] Let $\\rho:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^1$ with compact support contained in $\\overline B_R$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-metric-ball]]), put $\\varphi:=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$ and $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$. Then $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ ([[thm-newtonian-potential-for-holder-data-is-classical]]), and pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$ $$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=0$$ with the operators of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]] and [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. Moreover, with $Q:=\\int\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3$, for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the absolutely convergent Coulomb integral representation holds, and its far-field bound holds for $|x|>2R$, $$E(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{x-y}{|x-y|^3}\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\quad(x\\in\\mathbb R^3),\\qquad |E(x)|\\le\\frac{\\| \\rho\\|_1}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2}\\quad(|x|>2R),$$ and for $|x|>\\max(2R,1)$ the potential obeys the decay bound $|\\varphi(x)|\\le\\bigl(|Q|/(4\\pi)+R\\|\\rho\\|_1/\\pi\\bigr)/(\\varepsilon_0|x|)$ ([[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]], [[thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data]]). The pair $(E,0,\\rho,0)$ therefore realises the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with source $\\rho$, and $E$ is the field of the localised Newtonian potential. (b) [Uniqueness with vanishing at infinity] If $\\psi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfies $-\\Delta\\psi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}\\psi(x)=0$, then $\\psi=\\varphi$ ([[thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-the-laplacian]]). In particular, among $C^2$ solutions of the Poisson equation of a compactly supported $C^1$ source decaying at infinity, the localised Newtonian potential is the unique one. (c) [Compactly supported $L^1$ sources] If instead $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ is real, measurable and compactly supported, then $N\\rho$ is finite $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere, belongs to $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$, is $C^\\infty$ and harmonic off the support of $\\rho$, and its regular distribution satisfies $$-\\Delta T_{\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho}=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$ ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]], scaled by $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$; [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]). (d) [Point charge] Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and define, with arbitrary values at $x_0$, $$\\varphi_q(x):=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|},\\qquad E_q(x):=\\frac{q\\,(x-x_0)}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|^3}\\qquad(x\\ne x_0).$$ Then $\\varphi_q\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$, $E_q$ is locally integrable and $C^\\infty$ off $x_0$, $E_q=-\\nabla\\varphi_q$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{x_0\\}$, and, with $T_{\\varphi_q}$ the regular distribution of $\\varphi_q$ and $\\operatorname{div}E_q:=\\sum_i\\partial_iT_{E_{q,i}}$, $\\operatorname{curl}E_q:=(\\partial_2T_{E_{q,3}}-\\partial_3T_{E_{q,2}},\\ \\partial_3T_{E_{q,1}}-\\partial_1T_{E_{q,3}},\\ \\partial_1T_{E_{q,2}}-\\partial_2T_{E_{q,1}})$, $$-\\Delta T_{\\varphi_q}=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\delta_{x_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{div}E_q=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\delta_{x_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E_q=0\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$ ([[thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac]] for the first identity, [[lem-em-distributional-gradient-smooth-off-a-point]] for the passage from the potential to the field, [[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]])",
