     "source_section": "Statement",
     "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$). Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be a bounded open set, let $N\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be an open neighbourhood of its closure, let $\\kappa>0$, and let $A:N\\to V=\\mathbb R^4$ and $J:N\\to V$ be smooth ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Use the Minkowski form $\\eta$ and the index conventions of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]]: $A^\\mu$ denotes the components of $A$ in the standard basis, $A_\\nu:=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}A^\\mu$, $F_{\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, $F^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}\\eta^{\\nu\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta}$, and $J^\\nu A_\\nu$ is the indicated contraction of the components of $J$ with the covector $A_\\nu$. Put $L:=-\\frac{1}{4\\kappa}F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu A_\\nu$ and $S[A]:=\\int_U L\\,d\\lambda$, the $4$-dimensional Lebesgue integral of $L$ over $U$. Then $S[A]$ is a finite real number, and for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;V)$, with $h_\\nu:=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}h^\\mu$ and $G_{\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu h_\\mu$: (a) the map $s\\mapsto S[A+sh]$ is the quadratic polynomial $S[A]+s\\int_U\\ell_1\\,d\\lambda+s^2\\int_U\\ell_2\\,d\\lambda$, where $\\ell_1:=-\\kappa^{-1}F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu+J^\\nu h_\\nu$ and $\\ell_2:=-\\frac{1}{4\\kappa}G_{\\mu\\nu}G^{\\mu\\nu}$, so that its derivative at $s=0$ is $\\int_U\\bigl(-\\kappa^{-1}F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu+J^\\nu h_\\nu\\bigr)d\\lambda$; (b) that first variation also equals $\\int_U\\bigl(\\kappa^{-1}\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu\\bigr)h_\\nu\\,d\\lambda$; (c) $A$ is stationary for $S$, meaning that the derivative in (a) vanishes for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;V)$, if and only if $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\kappa J^\\nu$ for every $\\nu$ on $U$, all derivatives being classical partial derivatives of the smooth field $A$.",
